| `FAST_TERRAIN.zip` | Slope, aspect, hillshade, roughness, TRI, TPI | Figure 3.5 | Expanded command (§3.10.1) | Six supplied terrain rasters |
| `FAST_STRUCTURE.zip` | Core metrics, vertical metrics, geometry | Figures 3.6–3.8 | Expanded command (§3.10.1) | 36 supplied structure rasters |
| Other sampling techniques | Random/continuous/rough-height/imported | Schematic shape and sampling diagrams | Commands in §§3.4–3.6 | Source-supported examples, not benchmarked outputs |

## 3.18 References

The following sources were checked against publisher, professional-society, university, or institutional records. They support scientific interpretation and historical context; **they are not automatic evidence that FAST-GC uses their exact algorithms**.

1. **[R1]** Shannon, C. E., and Weaver, W. (1949). *The Mathematical Theory of Communication*. University of Illinois Press. [Bibliographic record](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog/101639117).
2. **[R2]** MacArthur, R. H., and Horn, H. S. (1969). Foliage profile by vertical measurements. *Ecology*, **50**(5), 802–804. https://doi.org/10.2307/1933693.
3. **[R3]** Lefsky, M. A., Cohen, W. B., Acker, S. A., Parker, G. G., Spies, T. A., and Harding, D. (1999). Lidar remote sensing of the canopy structure and biophysical properties of Douglas-fir western hemlock forests. *Remote Sensing of Environment*, **70**(3), 339–361. [Publisher article](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0034425799000528).
4. **[R4]** McGaughey, R. J. *FUSION/LDV: LiDAR Data Analysis and Visualization Software*. USDA Forest Service, Pacific Northwest Research Station. [Official software overview](https://forsys.sefs.uw.edu/FUSION/fusion_overview.html). Software documentation; no journal publication date asserted.
5. **[R5]** Zhang, K., Chen, S.-C., Whitman, D., Shyu, M.-L., Yan, J., and Zhang, C. (2003). A progressive morphological filter for removing nonground measurements from airborne LIDAR data. *IEEE Transactions on Geoscience and Remote Sensing*, **41**(4), 872–882. https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2003.810682.
6. **[R6]** Popescu, S. C., Wynne, R. H., and Nelson, R. F. (2002). Estimating plot-level tree heights with lidar: local filtering with a canopy-height based variable window size. *Computers and Electronics in Agriculture*, **37**(1–3), 71–95. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0168-1699(02)00121-7.
7. **[R7]** Popescu, S. C., and Wynne, R. H. (2004). Seeing the trees in the forest: using lidar and multispectral data fusion with local filtering and variable window size for estimating tree height. *Photogrammetric Engineering & Remote Sensing*, **70**(5), 589–604. https://doi.org/10.14358/PERS.70.5.589.
8. **[R8]** Weinmann, M., Jutzi, B., Hinz, S., and Mallet, C. (2015). Semantic point cloud interpretation based on optimal neighborhoods, relevant features and efficient classifiers. *ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing*, **105**, 286–304. [Bibliographic search](https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Weinmann+Jutzi+Hinz+Mallet+2015+semantic+point+cloud+interpretation).
9. **[R9]** Wilson, J. P., and Gallant, J. C. (eds.) (2000). *Terrain Analysis: Principles and Applications*. John Wiley & Sons. ISBN 9780471321880. [Publisher](https://www.wiley-vch.de/en/areas-interest/natural-sciences/geography-11ge/gis-remote-sensing-cartography-11ge6/terrain-analysis-978-0-471-32188-0).
10. **[R10]** Riley, S. J., DeGloria, S. D., and Elliot, R. (1999). A terrain ruggedness index that quantifies topographic heterogeneity. *Intermountain Journal of Sciences*, **5**(1–4), 23–27. [Journal record](https://arc.lib.montana.edu/ojs/index.php/IJS/article/view/2914).
11. **[R11]** Tarboton, D. G. (1997). A new method for the determination of flow directions and upslope areas in grid digital elevation models. *Water Resources Research*, **33**(2), 309–319. https://doi.org/10.1029/96WR03137.

